export const body = "<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>Collision detection is a system that calculates all interactions in a game. Collision means <em>making contact</em> while detection means <em>identifying</em>. By that logic, collision detection is a system that<strong> identifies every instant in which two objects make contact</strong>, then <strong>calculate the outcome based on the properties of the two objects</strong>.</p>\n<p>For example, when a character makes contact with a wall, the outcome is he cannot pass through it. Of course, this outcome is defined by programmers, which means if they allow the character to phase through it, they can make it happen.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/2219b6ac45f3e9deddb0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/2219b6ac45f3e9deddb0.webp\" width=\"694\" height=\"438\" style=\"--source-width:282px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/f82eec42593c363a11ea.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/f82eec42593c363a11ea.webp\" width=\"996\" height=\"465\" style=\"--source-width:285px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/a46060aa9c97efbf6b11.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/a46060aa9c97efbf6b11.webp\" width=\"905\" height=\"507\" style=\"--source-width:285px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Collision detection often takes the form of a box or a sphere, because it is more efficient to calculate a simple volume than a myriad of polygons on a model.</p>\n<p>This is the reason why clipping on character models is virtually unavoidable in video games: the only way to eliminate it entirely is to detect the collision of every polygon on the models.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>To expand on collision usage in navigation, we will look at <em>Uncharted 4</em>. In this game, the protagonist Nathan Drake will automatically put his hand on a nearby wall when you move close enough to it. This feature makes the character feel more connected to the environment around him.</p>\n<p>During the Madagascar level, you will encounter a lot of crowds, which means you will bump into other people very often. And as you might've guessed, Nathan Drake has specific animations for this kind of interaction.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/be7cef01eee7ffb7270d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/be7cef01eee7ffb7270d.webp\" width=\"699\" height=\"390\" style=\"--source-width:282px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/afbff9e3775083bcd52a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/afbff9e3775083bcd52a.webp\" width=\"643\" height=\"355\" style=\"--source-width:282px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/68add0427e9acd066de8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/68add0427e9acd066de8.webp\" width=\"806\" height=\"557\" style=\"--source-width:285px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Now, we will look at collision detection in fighting games, which require precise calculations in a matter of seconds.</p>\n<p>In the opposite picture, you can see a red box covering part of the character's leg. That is the hurtbox. If that box connects to any hitbox (yellow) on the opponent's, the damage is registered.Fun fact</p>\n<p>In<em> Resident Evil 2 (1998)</em>, you can unlock an extra mode where you play as a literal, walking piece of tofu. (Picture from the 2019 remake.)</p>\n<p>Originally, this comical model was a collision box, but during the development of the game, someone said it looked like a piece of tofu, and the rest of the team amused the idea. So they turned it into a reality.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/661144d02cc49183131d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/661144d02cc49183131d.webp\" width=\"569\" height=\"313\" style=\"--source-width:282px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>In the opposite picture, we see the collision mesh of a game level. The different colors in the mesh denote different interactions: yellow-green means the mesh can be walked on, pink means the mesh allows characters to lean on, and teal means they can duck behind it.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/bb59df14e2329c19bae6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/bb59df14e2329c19bae6.webp\" width=\"1137\" height=\"641\" style=\"--source-width:282px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/5eb3185929e9d99a7fca.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/5eb3185929e9d99a7fca.webp\" width=\"763\" height=\"418\" style=\"--source-width:285px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Though invisible during gameplay, collision detection has visible effects on players' experience. So, next time when you are blocked by a non-existent wall, you know what to blame.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>";
